== rebuild_cost ==
Rebuild cost: How long it would take to rewrite the package from its tests. Shown as one engineer's working time, and as agent sittings: how many times an AI agent would have to start over with a fresh context window. One sitting means it fits in one go; over three means the package has to be split before anyone can rebuild it. The sittings are measured on agent rebuilds and lead; packages are ranked by how often they changed times their agent passes, then by engineer time. Engineer time is a textbook figure, not a measurement: COCOMO II, rebuild profile, 2.381 person-months per thousand lines to the power 1.053 before each package's complexity rating, 19 working days a month, inflated by up to half where exports have no tests, summed over the packages. The agent sittings were measured on 85 rebuilds. The ratings under rebuild.cocomo in the configuration set the engineer time.
== split ==
Split: The package is past what one agent pass can hold, or over two of its limits. Carve it into smaller packages before adding to it.
== watch ==
Watch: Over one limit, large enough to need a few passes, or a quarter or more of it copied. Plan a split or a clean-up before it grows.
== copied ==
Copied: The share of lines inside duplicate blocks of 40 or more tokens. Every copy is a second place a fix has to land.
== untested ==
Untested: Exported functions no test touches. A rebuild would have to guess their behaviour.
== changes ==
Changes: Commits touching the package in the last 200. Hot code is where a split pays back soonest.
== limits ==
Limits: Per package: 1,000 lines, 16,000 tokens, a 600-line largest file, 60 exports, 10 internal imports. Fitted from well-regarded Go modules.
== hard_to_follow ==
Hard to follow: Each function gets a cognitive complexity score: how much branching and nesting a reader has to hold in their head. Up to 10 is easy, 11 to 20 needs care, over 20 is hard and the usual linter limit. "Paths" is the cyclomatic count, the number of distinct routes through the function a test would have to cover.
== paths ==
Hard to follow: Each function gets a cognitive complexity score: how much branching and nesting a reader has to hold in their head. Up to 10 is easy, 11 to 20 needs care, over 20 is hard and the usual linter limit. "Paths" is the cyclomatic count, the number of distinct routes through the function a test would have to cover.
